# .vercelignore — what does NOT get uploaded to a Vercel deployment.
# Same syntax as .gitignore. Vercel always skips node_modules/, .git/ and the
# build cache on its own; this file is only for things we exclude deliberately.
#
# ── The WhatsApp simulator's backend (v18.0.0 phase 5, Patryk's call) ─────────
# Vercel deploys api/ WHOLESALE — there is no build-time way to keep a handler
# out of a deployment — so without this line the three simulator endpoints ship
# to the restaurant's production project. They answer 404 there (simEnabled() is
# each handler's first statement and fails closed), so nobody could reach them;
# but the simulator is developer tooling and does not belong on a production
# deployment at all. "Cannot be run" and "is not there" are different claims,
# and this is the second one.
#
# It matters beyond tidiness: these three import firebase-admin, whose writes
# BYPASS the security rules entirely, and the Gemini client, which spends money.
# Deployed, they are one stray environment variable away from live. Not
# deployed, WA_SIM_ENABLED does nothing at all on production.
#
# ── The wa-sandbox branch MUST re-include them ───────────────────────────────
# The deployed sandbox calls these same-origin (`WA_BACKEND_URL` is "" outside
# the dev server), so excluding them there breaks its whole backend mode. The
# sandbox branch APPENDS one line below:
#
#     !api/wa-sim-*.js
#
# Appending rather than deleting the exclusion is deliberate: a prod-sync merge
# silently reinstates a deleted line — that is the failure
# `tests/wa-sandbox-integrity.test.js` exists to catch, after it happened twice —
# while an appended line survives the merge untouched. Check it after every sync.
api/wa-sim-*.js

# Claude's notes for this directory — not part of the deployment.
api/CLAUDE.md
